.TH LSMD "1" "March 2013" "lsmd @VERSION@" "libStorageMgmt plug-in daemon"
.SH NAME
Daemon \- lsmd
.SH DESCRIPTION
libStorageMgmt plug\-in daemon.  Plug-ins execute in their own process space
for fault isolation and to accommodate different plug\-in licensing
requirements.  The daemon normally runs as \fBroot\fR and drops privileges when
launching each plug\-in, only retaining root for plug\-ins that require it (see
\fBlsmd.conf\fR(5)).

.SH OPTIONS
\fB\-\-plugindir\fR = The directory where the plugins are located
.HP
\fB\-\-socketdir\fR = The directory for IPC sockets
.HP
\fB\-\-confdir\fR = The directory where the config file are located
.HP
\fB\-v\fR
= Verbose logging
.TP
\fB\-d\fR
= New style daemon (systemd) non-forking

.SH RESTRICTING SOCKET ACCESS
\fBlsmd\fR creates one Unix domain socket per plug\-in in the socket directory
(default \fB/run/lsm/ipc\fR, see \fB\-\-socketdir\fR). The individual socket
files are created world read/write, so which local users may connect to the
daemon is governed entirely by the permissions of the \fBsocket directory\fR,
not by the socket files themselves. Connecting to a socket requires search
(\fBx\fR) permission on the directory that contains it.

By default the directory is created with mode \fB0775\fR and owner
\fBroot:libstoragemgmt\fR, which lets any local user connect. Note that a
client's uid is only inspected (via \fBSO_PEERCRED\fR) to decide whether a
plug\-in may run with root privileges (see \fBlsmd.conf\fR(5)); it does not
restrict who may connect.

This matters on any machine with untrusted local users. \fBlsmd\fR forks and
execs a plug\-in process for every connection it accepts, before the client has
sent anything, so any user who can reach a socket can make the daemon fork. A
user sustaining connections can therefore keep the daemon busy spawning
workers; the \fBTasksMax\fR limit shipped in the systemd unit confines that to
the service's own cgroup, but the API stays unavailable for everyone until the
flood stops. Restricting the socket directory as described below is what
actually keeps an untrusted local user out.

\fBlsmd\fR caps how many plug\-in processes it will run concurrently for one
client uid (\fBmax-connections-per-uid\fR in \fBlsmd.conf\fR(5)). That keeps a
single user to a slice of the daemon's process budget rather than all of it,
but it does not stop a user who is permitted to connect from consuming their
slice, so it is no substitute for the directory permissions.

It also bounds how long a single worker in that slice can live before the
client authenticates (\fBplugin-registration-timeout\fR in \fBlsmd.conf\fR(5)),
so a connection that never completes \fBplugin_register\fR cannot occupy its
slot indefinitely.

To limit access, drop the \fBother\fR permission bits from the socket directory
and add the permitted users to the group that owns it. The directory must stay
readable, writable and searchable by \fBlsmd\fR itself: the daemon drops
privileges to the \fBlibstoragemgmt\fR user whenever no installed plug\-in
requires root (see \fBlsmd.conf\fR(5)), and it exits at start\-up if it cannot
access the socket directory, where it also keeps its lock file. So keep the
owning group \fBlibstoragemgmt\fR with its \fBrwx\fR bits intact and change
only the permissions granted to everyone else.

On systemd installations the directory is created at boot by
\fBsystemd-tmpfiles\fR(8); because \fB/run\fR is a \fBtmpfs\fR, changes made
directly with \fBchmod\fR(1) or \fBchown\fR(1) are lost on reboot. Make the
change persistent with a drop\-in in \fB/etc/tmpfiles.d/libstoragemgmt.conf\fR,
which overrides the packaged file in \fB/usr/lib/tmpfiles.d\fR:

    D /run/lsm 0775 root libstoragemgmt -
    D /run/lsm/ipc 0770 root libstoragemgmt -

Apply it without rebooting, then restart the daemon:

    # systemd-tmpfiles --create
    # systemctl restart libstoragemgmt.service

Then grant access to each permitted user by adding them to the group:

    # usermod -a -G libstoragemgmt \fIusername\fR

Restricting access to a single user is the same operation with a single member
in the group. Do not give the directory away to an individual user, or drop the
group's write permission: \fBlsmd\fR would no longer be able to create its
sockets and would fail to start. Note also that group members can write to the
socket directory, so grant membership only to users trusted not to disturb the
sockets themselves.

.SH SEE ALSO
\fIlsmd.conf\fR(5), \fIlsmcli\fR(1), \fIsystemd-tmpfiles\fR(8)

.SH BUGS
Please report bugs to
<libstoragemgmt-devel@lists.fedorahosted.org>
.SH AUTHOR
Tony Asleson <tasleson@redhat.com>

